1
00:00:00,878 --> 00:00:05,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Today's sutta is Samyutta Agama 636.

2
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:11,442
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, eh...

3
00:00:12,142 --> 00:00:21,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I've heard, one time the Buddha was staying at Kukuttarama Monastery in the city of Pataliputra.

4
00:00:23,486 --> 00:00:25,787
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, the World Honored One addressed the beakers.

5
00:00:26,368 --> 00:00:30,229
[SPEAKER_00]: I should explain to you the cultivation of the Four Establishments of Recollection.

6
00:00:31,934 --> 00:00:35,420
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the cultivation of the four establishments of recollection?

7
00:00:37,062 --> 00:00:42,872
[SPEAKER_00]: And... the Buddha said...

8
00:00:43,770 --> 00:01:08,835
[SPEAKER_00]: that when a tathāgata, an arahant, a perfectly awakened one, accomplished in knowledges and conduct, well gone, nowhere of the world, unsurpassed, guide of persons to be trained, teacher of the gods and humans, buddha, well-honored one, appears in the world, he proclaims the true dharma, excellent in the beginning, excellent in the middle, excellent in the end, good in the meaning and good in expression, pure and complete, revealing the brahmacharya, the holy life.

9
00:01:10,015 --> 00:01:14,899
[SPEAKER_00]: A young man of good family or a young woman of good family, hearing the Dharma from the Buddha, gains pure faith.

10
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:26,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Having cultivated in this way, seeing the faults of the household life with its desires and pleasures, seeing the bondage of the defilements, they delight in solitary dwellings.

11
00:01:26,969 --> 00:01:32,253
[SPEAKER_00]: They leave home to practice the path, no longer delighting in the household life.

12
00:01:33,221 --> 00:01:39,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Dwelling in homelessness, they aspire to complete purity and undertake the perfect and stainless brahmacharya for the whole life.

13
00:01:40,151 --> 00:01:49,043
[SPEAKER_00]: They think, I would shave off hair and beard, put on the saffron robe and out of faith go forth from the household life into homelessness to practice the path.

14
00:01:50,417 --> 00:01:56,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Having thought this, they abandon wealth and relatives, shave off their head and beard, put on saffron robe, out of faith go forth into homelessness.

15
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:05,743
[SPEAKER_00]: They straighten their bodily conduct, guard speech from the four faults, and purify their livelihood.

16
00:02:05,844 --> 00:02:12,226
[SPEAKER_00]: They practice the noble precepts, guard the sense faculties, and protect the mind with right recollection.

17
00:02:21,257 --> 00:02:26,318
[SPEAKER_00]: When the eye sees form, they do not grasp their general appearance, nor their particular features.

18
00:02:27,659 --> 00:02:37,702
[SPEAKER_00]: If dwelling with the eye-faculty unrestrained, worldly desires and distress and unwholesome states flow into the mind, then they cultivate the proper discipline regarding the eye-faculty.

19
00:02:38,642 --> 00:02:42,643
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same way, they cultivate proper discipline regarding the ear, nose, tongue, body and mind.

20
00:02:44,024 --> 00:02:48,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Having perfected the noble precepts and well-guarded the sense-faculties,

21
00:02:49,266 --> 00:03:10,372
[SPEAKER_00]: In going and coming, looking and turning, bending and stretching, in wearing robes, carrying the bowl, in eating, drinking, chewing, tasting, walking, standing, sitting, lying down, falling asleep and walking... twice... in speaking and remaining silent, they dwell clearly knowing and fully aware.

22
00:03:10,832 --> 00:03:17,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Having perfected such noble precepts, guarding the sense faculties, possessing the right recollection and the right knowing, they dwell secluded and ease in solitude.

23
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:22,809
[SPEAKER_00]: They sit alone in an empty place, at the root of a tree, or in a quiet dwelling.

24
00:03:23,089 --> 00:03:27,290
[SPEAKER_00]: They straighten the body, establish the recollection before them, and compose the citta.

25
00:03:28,231 --> 00:03:30,532
[SPEAKER_00]: They abandon worldly desires and distress.

26
00:03:31,872 --> 00:03:35,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Free from sensual desire, they purify the mind of sensual desire.

27
00:03:35,854 --> 00:03:39,835
[SPEAKER_00]: They abandon ill-will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, and doubt.

28
00:03:39,915 --> 00:03:40,815
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the five hindrances.

29
00:03:42,756 --> 00:03:47,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Free from ill-will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, doubt, they purify the mind of these states.

30
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:56,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, they abandon the five hindrances that corrupt the mind, weaken the wisdom, obstruct progress and do not lead to nirvana.

31
00:03:58,688 --> 00:04:07,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Having abandoned these five hindrances, they dwell contemplating the body in the body, internally, ardent, clearly knowing, mindful, having subdued worldly desires and distress.

32
00:04:10,036 --> 00:04:14,300
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same way, they contemplate the body externally, internally and externally.

33
00:04:15,022 --> 00:04:24,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So, likewise with feelings, with citta and dhammas, internally, externally, internally and externally, ardent, clearly knowing, recollected, having subdued worldly desires and distress.

34
00:04:27,273 --> 00:04:30,235
[SPEAKER_00]: This bhikkhu is called the cultivation of the four establishments of recollection.

35
00:04:30,876 --> 00:04:35,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and when the Buddha has spoken thus, the bhikkhus rejoiced in his words.

36
00:04:39,713 --> 00:04:46,622
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, it's not like a long sutta and, obviously, you can see a certain

37
00:04:48,268 --> 00:05:03,772
[SPEAKER_00]: amount of kind of parallels and similarity with the common Pali passages, such as, you know, gaining faith initially, on account of that, a person leaving the household life, and so on.

38
00:05:04,412 --> 00:05:17,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, what's interesting here, as we're going to see through, when we go through the questions I kind of asked, is that, well, the whole kind of four establishments of recollection, the satipaṭṭhānas,

39
00:05:18,488 --> 00:05:30,898
[SPEAKER_00]: These are clearly described as a very kind of a encompassing process, so to speak, that pretty much starts by leaving the house of life.

40
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:41,627
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's far from a retreat technique that you could just adopt on top of your existing life values and behavior.

41
00:05:42,681 --> 00:05:44,482
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the beginning of...

42
00:05:44,502 --> 00:05:47,003
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, what is the cultivation of the four establishments of recollection?

43
00:05:47,143 --> 00:05:54,245
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when Tathāgata arises in the world and a young man or a woman hear it and they gain faith in his teaching.

44
00:05:54,565 --> 00:05:57,766
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is actually, I think, found in Pali.

45
00:05:57,806 --> 00:06:00,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like this explicitly, but it's in combination.

46
00:06:00,988 --> 00:06:04,889
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's some Anguttara Nikāya, shorter suttas.

47
00:06:06,809 --> 00:06:21,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Where it's said that pretty much there is no... it's not possible to see body as body internally, internally, externally, or both, if one is not living brahmacharya, withdrawn from worldly desires and afflictions.

48
00:06:22,022 --> 00:06:30,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this sutta, in that regard, just kind of reiterates all those scattered statements throughout Pāli Nikaya.

49
00:06:31,309 --> 00:06:33,691
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, satipaṭṭhānas begin pretty much by

50
00:06:35,843 --> 00:06:38,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Undertaking the training in virtue correctly.

51
00:06:39,785 --> 00:07:07,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having cultivated this way, seeing the faults of the household life, in other words, what Pali sutras say, seeing that the household life is crowded, dusty, overwhelming, hard to maintain certain standards and training in that regard, because it's just the environment, it's not meant for it, that's why they would see the bondage and the defilements and start to delight in solitary dwellings and

52
00:07:09,251 --> 00:07:11,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning it all.

53
00:07:12,114 --> 00:07:31,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the... yeah, rather than being kind of going through the sutta technically and explaining the stuff that's self-evident, I thought of, well, discussing those kind of more like practical questions.

54
00:07:33,862 --> 00:07:49,556
[SPEAKER_00]: That a person, whether householder or a monk, can can still use, like today, to actually assess and re-evaluate and reflect on the practice at whatever stage they are in, so to speak.

55
00:07:51,638 --> 00:08:00,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said, yeah, technically speaking, the observing body in the body and the satipaṭṭhānas are towards the end of the sutta.

56
00:08:02,205 --> 00:08:26,802
[SPEAKER_00]: After the whole training in virtue, sense restraint, abandoning the desires and the griefs of the world, but as I said, from a certain angle you could say, well, that's already the beginning of satipaṭṭhāna, because it all leads to the discernment of the citta, the body for what it is, not as a kind of compilation of

57
00:08:27,738 --> 00:08:32,700
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, when you, like, read the sutta now, and you ask yourself, okay, so, what is mindfulness?

58
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:35,080
[SPEAKER_00]: When I, if I say mindfulness, mindfulness, what is mindfulness?

59
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:35,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Sati.

60
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:36,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's not even translate it.

61
00:08:36,121 --> 00:08:37,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's just say, what is sati?

62
00:08:58,153 --> 00:08:59,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask yourself that question.

63
00:09:01,097 --> 00:09:05,181
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would you answer?

64
00:09:05,782 --> 00:09:06,362
[SPEAKER_00]: What is sati?

65
00:09:06,483 --> 00:09:11,168
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, obviously, you'll give some... you might be giving some learned answer.

66
00:09:11,428 --> 00:09:14,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, sati is recollection, or it's this, and comes from the root of that word.

67
00:09:14,952 --> 00:09:18,076
[SPEAKER_00]: But, like, for you, here and now,

68
00:09:19,477 --> 00:09:29,729
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody were to ask you what is sati and you were to describe it, what phenomenon, palpable phenomenon, in your experience, here and now, would you be referring to?

69
00:09:31,011 --> 00:09:31,411
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

70
00:09:31,771 --> 00:09:38,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when you say sati, what is the actual phenomenon that you are designating?

71
00:09:40,185 --> 00:09:42,547
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you should be clear on that.

72
00:09:42,647 --> 00:09:43,327
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the point.

73
00:09:43,367 --> 00:09:51,533
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, you might have, as I said, a semi-accurate idea of what sati is, on account of learning, and practice, and so on.

74
00:09:52,794 --> 00:10:03,601
[SPEAKER_00]: But, when you say sati, even say, if you're not explaining it to anybody else, when you say, ah, sati, I should, I should practice mindfulness, what specific phenomenon are you referring to?

75
00:10:04,582 --> 00:10:05,463
[SPEAKER_00]: What sums it up?

76
00:10:05,703 --> 00:10:08,024
[SPEAKER_00]: What is its essence of the sati practice?

77
00:10:08,064 --> 00:10:08,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it that way.

78
00:10:10,426 --> 00:10:13,869
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is it something that you do?

79
00:10:13,949 --> 00:10:23,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it like a technique that you go through a certain set of motions or verbal kind of cues that bring it up?

80
00:10:25,218 --> 00:10:31,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it something you just directly kind of, say, access, recollect, re-establish?

81
00:10:33,644 --> 00:10:34,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way,

82
00:10:35,358 --> 00:10:36,819
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it in and of itself?

83
00:10:37,099 --> 00:10:37,960
[SPEAKER_00]: What is sati?

84
00:10:38,140 --> 00:10:40,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for you, how do you see it?

85
00:10:41,623 --> 00:10:45,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's a useful thing to ask yourself.

86
00:10:45,906 --> 00:10:51,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, and even if, by the way, if it feels clear, you should be checking in every now and then, because it might get obscured.

87
00:10:52,090 --> 00:11:02,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what tends to happen, practically speaking, is that, okay, you develop, as I said, some semi-accurate, more or less, doesn't matter, idea of sati,

88
00:11:04,282 --> 00:11:12,949
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a kind of a summary of everything you learned, everything you tried, you know, a bit of right, a bit of wrong, and so on.

89
00:11:14,270 --> 00:11:30,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're operating under that imperative of I must be mindful and do this practice that kind of contains a certain degree of, as I said, moving complexity and moving parts of sati practice like this, in this scenario, doing this, body in the body...

90
00:11:31,682 --> 00:11:53,269
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like... but it's not seen as a single unifying phenomenon that reestablish everything correctly, pretty much instantaneously, when recollected or when put forward, whichever way you would choose to translate the Pārimukha from the different suttas.

91
00:11:54,529 --> 00:11:56,570
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's important to dwell on this.

92
00:11:57,818 --> 00:12:03,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you're not looking for a pleasingly theoretical, all-encompassing answer that would cover all angles.

93
00:12:04,839 --> 00:12:08,820
[SPEAKER_00]: You're actually looking to see that phenomenon that you're talking about.

94
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,441
[SPEAKER_00]: When you say, yeah, mindfulness, or sati, or recollection, or recollectedness.

95
00:12:14,541 --> 00:12:15,121
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

96
00:12:15,241 --> 00:12:19,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when I say that, what am I thinking of?

97
00:12:19,602 --> 00:12:20,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I see that clearly?

98
00:12:24,751 --> 00:12:28,254
[SPEAKER_00]: And you start questioning that and then you'll see there's going to be a lot of layers to it.

99
00:12:29,415 --> 00:12:33,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's why it can be obviously very misleading as well.

100
00:12:35,621 --> 00:12:48,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I have no doubt that people without prior restraint and without simplification of their life, that this would list as a prerequisite to any practice of sati, they would still have some notion of sati.

101
00:12:49,692 --> 00:12:57,134
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like an entirely divorced concept that they cannot even fathom, unless they're celibate and practice, like... No, they can.

102
00:12:57,234 --> 00:12:58,134
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem.

103
00:12:58,874 --> 00:13:09,637
[SPEAKER_00]: It's whatever level of that phenomenon you can fathom, whatever idea you get, unless you're told that it won't be the sati you need to aim for, it will be the sati for you.

104
00:13:09,777 --> 00:13:11,117
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you won't be looking further.

105
00:13:11,177 --> 00:13:15,238
[SPEAKER_00]: You will think, oh, that is the sati, because, again, it matches what the sutta says.

106
00:13:15,518 --> 00:13:17,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, it's recollection, embodying the body.

107
00:13:18,099 --> 00:13:19,179
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not...

108
00:13:19,919 --> 00:13:34,546
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too difficult to wrap your head around these practices, but it's difficult to not fall into a trap whereby you stop recognizing or at least questioning whether there is more in it.

109
00:13:35,647 --> 00:13:41,710
[SPEAKER_00]: And obviously there is more in it, you know, if people start actually studying the whole body of the suttas and the āgamas and so on.

110
00:13:43,092 --> 00:13:46,895
[SPEAKER_00]: A clear picture emerges of, you know, how far you need to go and what you need to look for.

111
00:13:46,955 --> 00:14:05,272
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said before, most people would not necessarily undertake the level of that kind of study and practice, but it would more like be coming across the suttas here and there and second-hand accounts, and then very easily falling into the idea of, oh yeah, practice of mindfulness and recollection, I know what it is.

112
00:14:05,352 --> 00:14:09,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they do know what it is at that time, to that extent, at that level.

113
00:14:11,057 --> 00:14:14,938
[SPEAKER_00]: But if they were told to then also question it, like, oh, but is that it?

114
00:14:15,638 --> 00:14:17,839
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it specifically that I'm referring to?

115
00:14:18,259 --> 00:14:28,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just, like, as I said, a combination of kind of semi-techniques, ideas, theories, a bit of, kind of, informed by some practical attempts.

116
00:14:28,601 --> 00:14:29,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, no, no.

117
00:14:29,181 --> 00:14:32,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Specifically, when I say sati, what, what is it?

118
00:14:32,542 --> 00:14:33,343
[SPEAKER_00]: What state of mind?

119
00:14:33,383 --> 00:14:36,683
[SPEAKER_00]: What attitude towards the mind I'm referring to?

120
00:14:36,743 --> 00:14:38,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in its kind of essence.

121
00:14:39,504 --> 00:14:46,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Without any secondary psychologizations, rationalizations, explanations or secondary cues.

122
00:14:50,228 --> 00:15:01,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's like a... once you start questioning it, and, as I said, you're going to start discovering layers and layers to it.

123
00:15:02,293 --> 00:15:04,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, one of the most obvious layers

124
00:15:07,327 --> 00:15:13,954
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, when you start containing your worldly desires, not blindly following, and certainly not justifying them through your views,

125
00:15:29,667 --> 00:15:31,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying, oh, there's no harm in sensuality.

126
00:15:31,349 --> 00:15:32,130
[SPEAKER_00]: I can handle it.

127
00:15:32,831 --> 00:15:47,608
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you are, say, not able to give them up, you're at least on the level of views, maintaining the recognition that they're unbeneficial, obstructing, hindering and taking you away from the goal.

128
00:15:50,308 --> 00:16:11,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you start training in that direction, start containing your desires, start practicing sensory strength, containing of the animal, well, that's when you recognize that whole new, real, so to speak, dimension of sati, that kind of brought into play, that didn't exist before.

129
00:16:11,264 --> 00:16:15,627
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, you cannot know or reference what doesn't exist for you in the beginning.

130
00:16:15,667 --> 00:16:16,568
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of the point.

131
00:16:17,376 --> 00:16:17,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

132
00:16:17,636 --> 00:16:22,680
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like you need to be doing it to see that there is more to it.

133
00:16:23,640 --> 00:16:30,184
[SPEAKER_00]: And many suttas list those similes, many similes that explain exactly this.

134
00:16:30,505 --> 00:16:32,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like... that's why the whole

135
00:16:35,223 --> 00:16:42,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Brahmacharya, surmounting worldly, worldly desires, worldly worries, basically, entanglement with the world.

136
00:16:43,150 --> 00:16:52,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that, that needs to be the first point of practice of satipaṭṭhāna, of the Noble Eightfold Path or whatever, whichever way you want to designate it.

137
00:16:53,459 --> 00:16:57,082
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to begin by approximating the brahmacharya life.

138
00:16:57,943 --> 00:17:00,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you're a householder or a monk that

139
00:17:01,970 --> 00:17:09,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Training in virtue, sense restraint, guarding of the sense doors, subduing worldly desires and distress, i.e.

140
00:17:09,077 --> 00:17:16,264
[SPEAKER_00]: the level of involvement, intentional involvement with the world, is the first step of satipaṭṭhāna, is how you practice it.

141
00:17:19,026 --> 00:17:26,874
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, if your practice is not there yet, that you could sustain that type of lifestyle, well, you have work cut out for you.

142
00:17:28,183 --> 00:17:29,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Start working towards it.

143
00:17:29,504 --> 00:17:48,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Gradually, as I said, increasing saying no to the desires of the unwholesome, unbeneficial kind, until the mind will not be overwhelming you with panic, despair, anxiety, worry, at the mere thought of saying no to your central desire.

144
00:17:49,673 --> 00:18:03,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, as the other sutta says, like, initially, when a person not versed or trained in dhamma hears about sense restraint and saying no to their sensual and sexual desires, it feels like a sudden abyss, sudden precipice.

145
00:18:03,346 --> 00:18:05,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, everything was fine and suddenly you just reach the cliff.

146
00:18:06,048 --> 00:18:09,552
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how quickly that dread arises.

147
00:18:10,860 --> 00:18:11,521
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's normal.

148
00:18:12,162 --> 00:18:17,249
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... that just shows you the state of the wilderness of that animal.

149
00:18:17,609 --> 00:18:22,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, okay, so instead of despairing on account of it or trying to, like, as I said...

150
00:18:25,782 --> 00:18:30,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Rationalize it away through whatever views and stances and justifications.

151
00:18:31,244 --> 00:18:34,184
[SPEAKER_00]: You take it on as a measure of what needs to be developed.

152
00:18:34,284 --> 00:18:34,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

153
00:18:34,644 --> 00:18:45,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, when I think about sense restraint, when I think about approximating brahmacharya in a daily life and beginning the actual practice of satipaṭṭhāna correctly according to the suttas and āgamas, my animal freaks out.

154
00:18:46,967 --> 00:18:48,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Citta just freaks out.

155
00:18:49,008 --> 00:18:49,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

156
00:18:50,070 --> 00:18:55,392
[SPEAKER_00]: It's obviously too much too soon, but the direction remains outlined.

157
00:18:55,532 --> 00:19:01,875
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me begin slowly building it up and making it sort of become used to it.

158
00:19:03,175 --> 00:19:09,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, the only reason it's a sudden precipice is because the whole direction has been ignored and pretended it doesn't exist.

159
00:19:12,448 --> 00:19:21,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why approximating brahmacharya in daily life doesn't mean like overnight you need to become perfectly celibate and meet all the requirements, but it means you need to start working towards it.

160
00:19:22,738 --> 00:19:33,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Bit by bit, wherever you are recognizing the excess, the unnecessity of your intentional, volitional,

161
00:19:34,611 --> 00:19:36,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Engagement with centrality, proliferation.

162
00:19:37,013 --> 00:19:38,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you start curbing it.

163
00:19:38,674 --> 00:19:39,654
[SPEAKER_00]: You start saying no.

164
00:19:39,714 --> 00:19:51,421
[SPEAKER_00]: You start enduring bits and bits of those... of the... of the upsets and panics and little abysses that your animal will be coming up with until it calms down.

165
00:19:51,461 --> 00:19:59,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, the only reason it feels so, like, powerful and overwhelming is because it's a new thing.

166
00:19:59,266 --> 00:20:02,088
[SPEAKER_00]: It just hasn't been presented before and, obviously,

167
00:20:03,393 --> 00:20:10,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Capturing a wild animal suddenly will obviously reveal how wild it is and how much it resists and hates it.

168
00:20:10,978 --> 00:20:12,820
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eventually, it comes down.

169
00:20:13,020 --> 00:20:13,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's kind of the point.

170
00:20:14,661 --> 00:20:19,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would be closer to practicing satipaṭṭhāna correctly by training in virtue and

171
00:20:22,825 --> 00:20:46,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, by not doing that, or doing it conditionally with an expiry date for 10 days or 2 weeks or something, knowing that you would go back, and in the meantime do all these techniques of body scanning and whatnot, which has nothing, obviously, to do with the actual practice of satipaṭṭhāna that the suttas describe as the one way only that leads to purification.

172
00:20:47,672 --> 00:20:47,932
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

173
00:20:53,451 --> 00:20:55,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, I addressed that one, basically.

174
00:20:57,414 --> 00:21:02,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, like, there's a there's a thing that occurs a number of times in the sutta, and Pāli is as well mentioning.

175
00:21:02,118 --> 00:21:08,923
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, kind of, if I remember, Pāli mentions the equivalent of subduing worldly desires and distress.

176
00:21:10,745 --> 00:21:13,047
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he mentions it as something that's already been done.

177
00:21:14,492 --> 00:21:24,337
[SPEAKER_00]: In the Satipaṭṭhāna suttas, basically, or suttas that mention Satipaṭṭhāna, it was always mentioned as having dealt with that, having abandoned worldly joys and worries, or something like that.

178
00:21:24,357 --> 00:21:25,898
[SPEAKER_00]: I forgot the common translation.

179
00:21:26,579 --> 00:21:30,701
[SPEAKER_00]: He then dwells peaceful, withdrawn, here and now.

180
00:21:31,341 --> 00:21:36,144
[SPEAKER_00]: This āgama is kind of nice, because it includes a bit of a bit of nuance in between,

181
00:21:36,964 --> 00:21:48,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, before a person is... a monk or a nun is dwelling happily in seclusion, purifying their mind from unwholesome states, they are making the effort subduing worldly desires and distress.

182
00:21:49,889 --> 00:22:02,254
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you trap the animal, the worldly desires, the desires of the prior, access to the wilderness and things they were causing you, joys, happiness, worry or distress, will keep coming up.

183
00:22:03,815 --> 00:22:06,496
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you go about subduing it?

184
00:22:07,843 --> 00:22:09,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the question again.

185
00:22:09,424 --> 00:22:10,885
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be another reflection.

186
00:22:10,925 --> 00:22:15,668
[SPEAKER_00]: You ask yourself every now and then.

187
00:22:16,968 --> 00:22:27,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Say you live withdrawn to the best extent you are able to at the time from unwholesome, unbeneficial desires and pursuits and so on.

188
00:22:28,555 --> 00:22:34,118
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, you know, the untamed mind still comes up with pressures, desires, distress.

189
00:22:35,639 --> 00:22:36,940
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how are you subduing that?

190
00:22:38,964 --> 00:22:39,604
[SPEAKER_00]: What does that mean?

191
00:22:41,065 --> 00:22:47,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it mean hypnotizing the mind to the point of no thoughts coming up?

192
00:22:47,887 --> 00:22:49,588
[SPEAKER_00]: It's peaceful because it's asleep.

193
00:22:51,308 --> 00:22:52,469
[SPEAKER_00]: But is that truly a peace?

194
00:22:53,369 --> 00:22:59,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, take the simile of the wild animal that you're taming.

195
00:23:01,912 --> 00:23:05,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Does the real peace come when animal is just temporarily asleep?

196
00:23:07,030 --> 00:23:16,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Or would the real peace come from when an animal is incapable of attacking you, regardless of whether it's awake, sleeping, agitated or not?

197
00:23:17,178 --> 00:23:21,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the actual, more foundational peace?

198
00:23:23,924 --> 00:23:26,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, when the question is asked like that, you already know the answer.

199
00:23:27,347 --> 00:23:28,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's kind of the point.

200
00:23:29,188 --> 00:23:35,634
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like that you shouldn't be trying to, like, well, calm down the animal, if it helps.

201
00:23:37,318 --> 00:23:39,880
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be doing it whether you want it or not, one way or the other.

202
00:23:40,401 --> 00:23:48,286
[SPEAKER_00]: But you should certainly not fall into a view that artificially calming down the animal because it just scares me too much is the way of practice.

203
00:23:48,366 --> 00:23:48,547
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

204
00:23:49,167 --> 00:24:00,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Teaching the animal, making it understand these things that you emulate through your own behavior and right choices by body, speech and mind, that is what will calm it.

205
00:24:01,156 --> 00:24:02,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Actual samādhi.

206
00:24:02,757 --> 00:24:03,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Actual samatha.

207
00:24:04,884 --> 00:24:14,447
[SPEAKER_00]: whereby, it doesn't matter what state the animal is in, because you know that it cannot turn against you, because it has been sufficiently tamed.

208
00:24:15,767 --> 00:24:19,968
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what the subduing of worldly desires and distress has to pertain to.

209
00:24:20,749 --> 00:24:29,171
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when things concern with the world, desires and so on, yes, you say no to the outpours through your behavior, so you don't act out by body, by speech, by mind,

210
00:24:30,285 --> 00:24:31,686
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not where it ends.

211
00:24:31,726 --> 00:24:40,512
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like now you just spend time wishfully thinking that the animal will never remember again those things that used to, eh... spur desires in it, or distress.

212
00:24:40,652 --> 00:24:41,032
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

213
00:24:41,893 --> 00:24:43,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, okay, so there is no acting out.

214
00:24:43,534 --> 00:24:44,534
[SPEAKER_00]: I've contained the animal.

215
00:24:45,615 --> 00:24:50,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I actually have to, eh... reflect on these things correctly.

216
00:24:50,458 --> 00:24:51,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Bring them up.

217
00:24:51,259 --> 00:24:52,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Bring their counterparts up.

218
00:24:53,220 --> 00:24:57,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Broaden the context, so when those thoughts do pop up, which they inevitably will,

219
00:24:58,990 --> 00:25:02,133
[SPEAKER_00]: They won't limit the mind anymore, in the sense they used to.

220
00:25:02,233 --> 00:25:07,998
[SPEAKER_00]: They won't be immediately called... inflict kind of narrowing of the focus of the animal.

221
00:25:08,038 --> 00:25:11,000
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, they will be less and less of a trigger.

222
00:25:11,460 --> 00:25:12,441
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's still the same thing.

223
00:25:13,422 --> 00:25:16,745
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same provocation, but comes with a different background.

224
00:25:18,092 --> 00:25:19,593
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that whole...

225
00:25:19,693 --> 00:25:45,347
[SPEAKER_00]: I keep talking about, you know, the context, the broadening of the context, the right perceptions, bringing them up in a controlled, suitable, virtue-based environment, so that animal relearns to not just immediately fall for the trap, but not lose the sight of the broader picture, even when it's the most triggering things that come up.

226
00:25:52,560 --> 00:25:58,825
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, another practical thing to consider would be, like, when the sutta talks about the faults of the household life.

227
00:26:00,866 --> 00:26:06,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, now, there's a theoretical answer enumerating all the correct things, but more, like, for you.

228
00:26:07,671 --> 00:26:09,993
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and, by the way, again, it doesn't need to be household life.

229
00:26:10,113 --> 00:26:15,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Any current situation, you're gonna have a degree of

230
00:26:16,668 --> 00:26:22,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Distraction, compromising choices, it's just like to what extent do I allow that... do you allow that to go?

231
00:26:24,016 --> 00:26:30,783
[SPEAKER_00]: So, faults of household life could be faults of my current situation that are kind of self-inflicted, so to speak.

232
00:26:30,864 --> 00:26:35,649
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you look for the moments where you're giving in to hindrances.

233
00:26:37,003 --> 00:26:37,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Comfort.

234
00:26:38,604 --> 00:26:39,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Forms of entertainment.

235
00:26:40,004 --> 00:26:40,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Not...

236
00:26:40,844 --> 00:26:51,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, you're not judging or trying to get rid of the existence of those things or possibilities, but you're trying to see when your choice takes you there.

237
00:26:52,168 --> 00:26:54,549
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you willingly choose to go there.

238
00:26:56,612 --> 00:27:24,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you willingly choose to give in to unnecessary excessive comfort or like food or entertainment, when you're delighting in socializing, even if it's on the basis of obligations, for example, I have to, but it's like, okay, but are you doing it because you have to or I have to becomes a permission for I can now carelessly engage with this because it has to be done and stuff like that.

239
00:27:25,029 --> 00:27:28,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you need to do is, as I say, keep an eye on your choices.

240
00:27:30,298 --> 00:27:33,104
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the choice is clear, whether you're choosing that, you don't choose it.

241
00:27:33,845 --> 00:27:37,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do those things come up on their own and how often that becomes irrelevant?

242
00:27:38,205 --> 00:27:43,109
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the whole point is, like, you don't have to be fighting everything all the time, everything and nothing.

243
00:27:43,649 --> 00:27:45,811
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to recognize when the choices are clear.

244
00:27:46,251 --> 00:27:52,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, they have to be clear, because whenever you are making a choice, means there is a consciousness of choosing.

245
00:27:52,996 --> 00:27:55,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is the options that you are aware of.

246
00:27:55,078 --> 00:27:59,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, the choice would be... making a choice would be incomprehensible, it would be inconceivable.

247
00:28:00,531 --> 00:28:08,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the choosing is that kind of a point of convergence, so to speak.

248
00:28:08,994 --> 00:28:10,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you need to keep an eye on.

249
00:28:10,434 --> 00:28:12,835
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the faults become faults.

250
00:28:12,975 --> 00:28:14,756
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where hindrances become hindrances.

251
00:28:14,836 --> 00:28:16,496
[SPEAKER_00]: When you choose to take them up.

252
00:28:18,757 --> 00:28:20,277
[SPEAKER_00]: When you choose to act out of them.

253
00:28:21,578 --> 00:28:21,898
[SPEAKER_00]: And again,

254
00:28:23,076 --> 00:28:32,939
[SPEAKER_00]: People keep choosing, not because they didn't have sufficient amount of special novelty hypnotic experiences in their meditation or revelations.

255
00:28:33,080 --> 00:28:37,881
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because they haven't really thought about this frequently enough.

256
00:28:38,141 --> 00:28:51,206
[SPEAKER_00]: They haven't trained these reflections in order for them to sink in, to become, as I said, the informing context behind quick triggering desires, hindrances and so on.

257
00:28:52,725 --> 00:29:03,669
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like, you know, so many suttas, like frequently recollecting and dwelling on the suitable themes, they would then be resulting in dispassion and so on.

258
00:29:03,709 --> 00:29:11,172
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like a magical resolution, but it's just training and effort and diligence, really, nothing else.

259
00:29:14,133 --> 00:29:18,135
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it kind of ties in with the whole idea of guarding the sense doors.

260
00:29:19,030 --> 00:29:24,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you don't have to be a monk or a nun in order to guard the sense doors.

261
00:29:25,312 --> 00:29:26,413
[SPEAKER_00]: You can already be doing it.

262
00:29:27,053 --> 00:29:28,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you guard the sense doors?

263
00:29:29,934 --> 00:29:38,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you guard the sense doors after you chose to allow senses to look, smell, taste, touch unsuitable things?

264
00:29:38,297 --> 00:29:40,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is guarding of the sense doors done before that?

265
00:29:42,759 --> 00:29:46,660
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you first give in to the unwholesome choice and then try to repair it?

266
00:29:49,775 --> 00:29:54,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you able to not even give in to the unwholesome choice and then there is nothing to repair?

267
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:01,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, I already gave you an answer now, but the point is not the answer.

268
00:30:01,242 --> 00:30:05,945
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is that you bring those questions to yourself as often as possible.

269
00:30:07,296 --> 00:30:08,477
[SPEAKER_00]: That should be your meditation.

270
00:30:09,058 --> 00:30:11,261
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, start painting the bigger picture.

271
00:30:11,601 --> 00:30:12,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Bit by bit.

272
00:30:12,923 --> 00:30:26,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Every time you start these reflections, you bring these questions that I keep sort of listing and creating, that revolve around the same things but give you different angles, you are adding to your canvas of that context.

273
00:30:26,318 --> 00:30:27,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You are adding bit by bit.

274
00:30:29,293 --> 00:30:31,055
[SPEAKER_00]: No masterpiece is painted overnight.

275
00:30:31,715 --> 00:30:33,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just like... but it grows.

276
00:30:33,637 --> 00:30:34,218
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

277
00:30:34,338 --> 00:30:34,978
[SPEAKER_00]: It adds more.

278
00:30:35,098 --> 00:30:40,843
[SPEAKER_00]: What you've been questioning before, part of it becomes, as I said, embedded in that context now.

279
00:30:40,963 --> 00:30:52,393
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time you add more and more, and you don't have to keep revisiting everything from the start, because, as I said, it becomes part of that background that it's informing your current experience more and more accurately, so to speak.

280
00:30:52,413 --> 00:30:55,075
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much how you will be developing the right view as well.

281
00:30:58,659 --> 00:31:23,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, say a person does this, practices questioning, then the whole place for seclusion... We spoke about it in other videos, so I won't go into too much detail on that here, but... Let me just... Just checking if the zoom is still on.

282
00:31:25,356 --> 00:31:26,217
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the seclusion...

283
00:31:31,840 --> 00:31:44,705
[SPEAKER_00]: To whatever extent you're able to have, say, periods of seclusion, re-evaluate... you should re-evaluate as to, like, can you improve it.

284
00:31:46,286 --> 00:32:00,051
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, are there choices concerning my, you know, life, current living situation, duties and so on, that I could reorient, so to speak, so that I have more seclusion for practice.

285
00:32:02,963 --> 00:32:05,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, probably yes.

286
00:32:05,384 --> 00:32:10,347
[SPEAKER_00]: For most people, especially in a household life, that could be improved.

287
00:32:10,407 --> 00:32:18,770
[SPEAKER_00]: As for the monks, nuns, you realize, as I said, it can always be improved.

288
00:32:18,810 --> 00:32:32,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Not necessarily like you need to find a new place or something, but you would see that there is going to be at least some degree of your intentional involvements or engagements that would be taking you away from seclusion.

289
00:32:33,825 --> 00:32:35,286
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just speaking broadly, obviously.

290
00:32:36,286 --> 00:32:39,028
[SPEAKER_00]: It's up to every individual to assess that.

291
00:32:39,048 --> 00:32:43,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Because obviously, like, seclusion in and of itself, it's not gonna have magical results either.

292
00:32:43,770 --> 00:32:52,294
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to be doing things rightly, practicing things rightly, where seclusion can then be the actual useful thing.

293
00:32:52,754 --> 00:32:53,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah.

294
00:32:55,696 --> 00:32:57,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Re-evaluate at the end of your day.

295
00:32:58,860 --> 00:33:10,481
[SPEAKER_00]: When there were no, say, extracurricular activities that you had to attend, monastery projects or, I don't know, family visits in a household life and stuff,

296
00:33:11,648 --> 00:33:16,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Just reassess whether your choices are aligned with maximizing seclusion that you can get.

297
00:33:17,871 --> 00:33:21,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, not for sleeping or distracting yourself.

298
00:33:21,553 --> 00:33:25,555
[SPEAKER_00]: But for this, there's right practices, right reflections, right context.

299
00:33:26,315 --> 00:33:32,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you spend that seclusion walking, hiking, sitting, studying the suttas, doesn't matter.

300
00:33:32,258 --> 00:33:33,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be different times on different days.

301
00:33:33,979 --> 00:33:36,740
[SPEAKER_00]: But, like, are you physically alone?

302
00:33:38,301 --> 00:33:40,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Just you and your own mind, basically.

303
00:33:42,892 --> 00:33:52,097
[SPEAKER_00]: If a good part of your day, you could say, yeah, I mean, most of it is in seclusion, then you don't have to worry too much or micromanage, you know, every choice you make.

304
00:33:52,257 --> 00:34:02,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I shouldn't have talked about that, because, you know, five minutes longer or something, I could have had my... That's like, you know, becoming a bit precious and irrational about seclusion.

305
00:34:03,664 --> 00:34:10,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion just means time when the work and reflection is most efficient.

306
00:34:11,447 --> 00:34:14,509
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if you don't have seclusion, you can still do the work.

307
00:34:15,049 --> 00:34:16,450
[SPEAKER_00]: It just won't be as efficient.

308
00:34:17,611 --> 00:34:20,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to recognize that.

309
00:34:20,853 --> 00:34:23,215
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like I can only practice when I'm in seclusion.

310
00:34:23,255 --> 00:34:25,176
[SPEAKER_00]: If there's no seclusion, there is no practice.

311
00:34:25,256 --> 00:34:34,002
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, householders became anāgāmīs in the sūtas, with their duties and families and things they had to look after.

312
00:34:34,122 --> 00:34:35,763
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know,

313
00:34:36,923 --> 00:34:56,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously means they had to make more effort that would have been a lot more efficient if they were not householders, but the work is work, needs to be done and like, you know, I don't know, stuff can happen and you end up hospitalized or in an environment where you simply cannot practice seclusion.

314
00:34:56,809 --> 00:34:59,050
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of choices, right choices that you make.

315
00:35:01,290 --> 00:35:03,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you ready to practice in that environment?

316
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:10,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is your idea of practice just completely, kind of, merged with seclusion?

317
00:35:10,744 --> 00:35:13,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Without seclusion, it's just... there is no practice.

318
00:35:13,366 --> 00:35:19,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything I do, then, must be simply about bringing me back into seclusion, where I can practice.

319
00:35:19,550 --> 00:35:20,971
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not an accurate idea.

320
00:35:22,712 --> 00:35:33,940
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seclusion is certainly a very, say, useful prerequisite, but it's a relative thing.

321
00:35:36,308 --> 00:35:42,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the obvious seclusion that physical seclusion would be leading to is seclusion from unwholesome states.

322
00:35:43,670 --> 00:35:45,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion from greed, aversion, distraction.

323
00:35:45,971 --> 00:35:52,914
[SPEAKER_00]: But you are not united with greed, aversion, distraction through presence of other bodies and people and sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

324
00:35:53,454 --> 00:35:57,255
[SPEAKER_00]: You're united through your own ignorant choices towards those things.

325
00:35:58,569 --> 00:36:10,732
[SPEAKER_00]: So, physical seclusion can emulate the separation, but, as we already, I think, we did it in these studies as well, the actual seclusion, the only way to develop it, is from the inside, the withdrawal.

326
00:36:12,632 --> 00:36:28,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly how householders became anāgāmīs, like, overcome sensual desire, the five hindrances, the... sorry, the five lower fetters, while not being as secluded as monks, not even having an option to be so.

327
00:36:29,987 --> 00:36:33,928
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, they made the effort, applied the effort where it matters.

328
00:36:35,208 --> 00:36:49,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So, internally, they have secluded themselves from the hindrances, from the unwholesome states, to the point of where certain views and assumptions in regard to centrality or will just were evaporated, were destroyed.

329
00:36:54,173 --> 00:36:56,413
[SPEAKER_00]: And that kind of ties in with the following point.

330
00:37:00,537 --> 00:37:04,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the practical ways of addressing the five hindrances correctly throughout the day.

331
00:37:04,740 --> 00:37:05,461
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly it.

332
00:37:07,022 --> 00:37:19,411
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you're secluded or not, as I said, seclusion is an added bonus, but if, whatever, things come up that prevent your seclusion, do you then justify your annoyance?

333
00:37:20,325 --> 00:37:25,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel righteous in your ill-will because somebody has interrupted your all-important seclusion?

334
00:37:26,370 --> 00:37:30,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you take that interruption of seclusion as the practice to not give in to the five hindrances?

335
00:37:32,574 --> 00:37:35,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you have a choice what you're going to emphasize.

336
00:37:35,857 --> 00:37:40,500
[SPEAKER_00]: The world, the concern with the world, which becomes seclusion, is another concern with the world.

337
00:37:41,741 --> 00:37:44,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the internal giving in to the five hindrances.

338
00:37:44,383 --> 00:37:46,645
[SPEAKER_00]: In this instance would be irritation and ill-will.

339
00:37:51,925 --> 00:38:03,888
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, another kind of useful reference point, but again, I mean, I asked this, I added this to the end of the the self-questioning, because people can easily fall into technique of body watching and scanning.

340
00:38:04,448 --> 00:38:10,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... say you are reflecting correctly, you are making

341
00:38:11,794 --> 00:38:17,437
[SPEAKER_00]: The beneficial choices, saying no to the unbeneficial choices, containing the animal to the best of your ability.

342
00:38:18,538 --> 00:38:32,165
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're doing that, which is, you could say, the meat of the practice, of the right practice, how often or are you simultaneous to whatever you're rightly practicing?

343
00:38:33,086 --> 00:38:40,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you simultaneously aware with simply the state of your body as a whole, in your situation?

344
00:38:42,229 --> 00:38:42,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it seated?

345
00:38:43,130 --> 00:38:43,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it walking?

346
00:38:43,750 --> 00:38:49,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Just like... do you see it as a... like, say, you're bringing up... like this now, where we spoke about seclusion.

347
00:38:49,555 --> 00:38:53,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're making sure you don't fall into a trap or becoming precious about seclusion.

348
00:38:53,618 --> 00:38:58,342
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're bringing up scenarios, you're reflecting, you're seeing if the animal becomes triggered on account of things it doesn't like.

349
00:38:59,583 --> 00:39:03,945
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, do you, like, allow it in a sense of, like, oh, yeah, yeah, and I should have done this?

350
00:39:04,005 --> 00:39:04,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, no, hold on.

351
00:39:05,125 --> 00:39:10,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything, everything can be a basis for the right practice, if the right effort is made correctly.

352
00:39:11,708 --> 00:39:22,012
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while you're doing these reflections, do you also, in that whole package, so to speak, see yourself seated on a chair in your room, while thinking these things?

353
00:39:22,632 --> 00:39:24,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, don't mystify too much.

354
00:39:25,133 --> 00:39:28,516
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't completely exclude it either, those recognitions.

355
00:39:29,237 --> 00:39:34,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, basically, those indirect ways... Should I tell you that now?

356
00:39:34,963 --> 00:39:36,865
[SPEAKER_00]: It might just become another technique people seek.

357
00:39:37,745 --> 00:39:38,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, I'm qualifying it now.

358
00:39:38,987 --> 00:39:41,289
[SPEAKER_00]: So, make sure it doesn't become another technique you seek.

359
00:39:42,610 --> 00:39:47,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, those indirect ways, when, say, you are

360
00:39:48,359 --> 00:39:59,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Enduring things rightly, not giving in to sensuality, training in virtue, guarding of the sense doors, indirectly seeing your body in those situations, is the most accurate way of seeing the phenomenon of kāyā.

361
00:40:00,607 --> 00:40:04,429
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not when you're directly attending it, feeling it, or body scanning it and stuff.

362
00:40:05,570 --> 00:40:13,215
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, see, trying to directly attend to those indirect ways of the way the kāyā is recognized,

363
00:40:14,816 --> 00:40:24,083
[SPEAKER_00]: You can start to emulate the indirect way, but it's actually rooted in your direct attention of gaining that mindfulness of the body or whatever you think it is.

364
00:40:24,103 --> 00:40:24,663
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I'm saying.

365
00:40:25,123 --> 00:40:36,451
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way... well, the most accurate way of practicing mindfulness of the body is when it's genuinely, indirectly seen in regard to whatever, say, you are rightly preoccupied with.

366
00:40:38,153 --> 00:40:40,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's training that indirect peripheral vision,

367
00:40:42,468 --> 00:40:53,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And making sure that it doesn't become your, as I said, covertly, direct way of indirectly, peripherally seeing it, because you think it's a magical method for some purification.

368
00:40:53,132 --> 00:40:53,272
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

369
00:40:53,793 --> 00:40:58,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Magical method for purification is making the right choices throughout your day.

370
00:40:59,535 --> 00:41:01,816
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is greed, non-greed, you choose non-greed.

371
00:41:01,876 --> 00:41:04,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Aversion, non-aversion, you choose non-aversion.

372
00:41:04,296 --> 00:41:06,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Distraction, non-distraction, you choose non-distraction.

373
00:41:06,497 --> 00:41:07,558
[SPEAKER_00]: By body, speech and mind.

374
00:41:07,918 --> 00:41:09,318
[SPEAKER_00]: To the best of your abilities.

375
00:41:09,759 --> 00:41:10,639
[SPEAKER_00]: The more you do it,

376
00:41:12,377 --> 00:41:14,898
[SPEAKER_00]: The clearer it will become what needs to be done.

377
00:41:17,039 --> 00:41:33,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the kind of last point is, is like, after, you know, everything we said, when you think, when you hear continuous, uninterrupted mindfulness, sati, that, you know, even the suttas mention,

378
00:41:35,646 --> 00:41:37,047
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it mean to you?

379
00:41:38,027 --> 00:41:39,307
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think that is?

380
00:41:39,788 --> 00:41:59,915
[SPEAKER_00]: And I ask that because, again, like, despite everything we say, and even people that say, actually listen and think about these things, when you hear about continuous mindfulness, you still might think it's like, oh, it's like a continuous focus of non-interrupted pristine awareness of the pure consciousness of the eternal citta, whatever.

381
00:42:00,787 --> 00:42:07,012
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like this kind of full laser beam focus all the time, omniscient in every direction.

382
00:42:07,172 --> 00:42:08,333
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like... is it?

383
00:42:11,256 --> 00:42:21,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, after everything you've said, the āgamas and these suttas and that suttas, and when we listed, you know, how satipaṭṭhāna is practiced, is that what continuous uninterrupted mindfulness would be?

384
00:42:24,487 --> 00:42:24,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Or not?

385
00:42:27,946 --> 00:42:29,787
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, sit with that question.

386
00:42:29,887 --> 00:42:32,449
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the continuous uninterrupted mindfulness?

387
00:42:33,450 --> 00:42:39,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it based on my determination to keep my focus aligned?

388
00:42:42,836 --> 00:42:48,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it an effortless result of doing things the right way, training the right way?

389
00:42:50,882 --> 00:42:51,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one is it?

390
00:42:51,942 --> 00:42:54,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, obviously, I'm answering it because it's not about specific answer.

391
00:42:54,904 --> 00:42:55,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's this or that.

392
00:42:56,005 --> 00:43:00,929
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like asking yourself to see where your implications rest.

393
00:43:01,690 --> 00:43:17,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you still expect uninterrupted sati to be, as I said, like a uninterrupted beam of awareness that instantaneously sees everything all the time as it comes and it goes?

394
00:43:18,903 --> 00:43:21,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you think continuous mindfulness is basically where

395
00:43:23,896 --> 00:43:38,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Emphasis cannot be reshifted back onto desire, ill-will, sloth, laziness, restlessness.

396
00:43:39,689 --> 00:43:46,895
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, those things will come up, but they don't shift the weight back onto them to become the center of your experience.

397
00:43:47,335 --> 00:43:50,518
[SPEAKER_00]: They remain as something that has come up, but contained.

398
00:43:52,315 --> 00:44:00,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that continuous mindfulness does not require you actively holding it together.

399
00:44:00,862 --> 00:44:03,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's obviously... you won't be mindful of that.

400
00:44:04,725 --> 00:44:06,907
[SPEAKER_00]: That's obviously narrowing of the perspective.

401
00:44:10,070 --> 00:44:19,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's good to ask these things, because, like, you know, everybody, you're gonna have some preconceived notions hiding here and there, little expectations, that, as I said, will be fueling

402
00:44:21,052 --> 00:44:27,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Certain expectations and modes of behavior that the only reason they do so, because you never really stopped and questioned them.

403
00:44:27,114 --> 00:44:38,199
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, hold on, I'm still acting as if I'm supposed to be, you know, mindful, like, in every direction, omnisciently, all the time, in regard to everything.

404
00:44:38,259 --> 00:44:39,879
[SPEAKER_00]: But that can't be the case.

405
00:44:43,401 --> 00:44:49,543
[SPEAKER_00]: If mindfulness is developed on account of... Satipaṭṭhāna begins with training in virtue and sense restraint.

406
00:44:51,225 --> 00:44:54,666
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that already is not something I'm doing all the time.

407
00:44:54,986 --> 00:44:59,527
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing it when the choice to go that way or not presents itself.

408
00:44:59,547 --> 00:45:00,868
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm choosing rightly.

409
00:45:00,928 --> 00:45:04,168
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm enduring the state of the animal that comes up as a result of that choice.

410
00:45:04,188 --> 00:45:04,989
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not giving in.

411
00:45:06,009 --> 00:45:07,289
[SPEAKER_00]: I wait for it to calm down.

412
00:45:07,309 --> 00:45:08,910
[SPEAKER_00]: I present it with the right perceptions.

413
00:45:09,470 --> 00:45:12,591
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's like, oh, so that's really the mindfulness.

414
00:45:12,671 --> 00:45:17,532
[SPEAKER_00]: When that... an extent of the context has been developed sufficiently,

415
00:45:18,502 --> 00:45:26,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, even without any effort from your part to bring the right context and contain the animal, the animal doesn't go back there.

416
00:45:26,429 --> 00:45:35,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, the big picture that you painted now is clear as a day, and whatever pops in the front, the animal cannot not see it.

417
00:45:37,038 --> 00:45:40,041
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the uninterrupted, continuous mindfulness.

418
00:45:40,782 --> 00:45:44,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the result of the... of building up the right practice.

